ish and empire"}, {"id": "c17718b8-1c8f-4acd-9b0c-4c00fd67eaca", "type": "concept", "title": "Creole"}, {"id": "a46c52a0-0a52-491e-b652-39218eef98fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Vernacular"}, {"id": "b438ec87-de9a-44a0-aac8-c4670584f8c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Code-switching"}], "description": "Gist: Students study how imperial, local, creole, Indigenous, and hybrid languages carry power."}, {"id": "90907c1c-c9cc-45a0-9115-d9455bf74746", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Form and linguistic choice", "children": [{"id": "d2db7cac-acf3-4d23-a4b4-bf2846151eea", "type": "concept", "title": "Glossing"}, {"id": "569f2c4e-b485-44ef-a64f-1c5538a8dc0b", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslated words"}, {"id": "fad905a2-5070-423b-be0e-23107414ee57", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice"}, {"id": "fae47799-426f-46a1-97d1-e0e81d9bb0cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Register"}], "description": "Gist: Language choices shape rhythm, characterization, audience, and political meaning."}]}, {"id": "7c238bce-8dc3-47f4-bb06-16785a7f7141", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Ethnicity, and Comparative Racialization", "description": "Compare how racial and ethnic categories are made, contested, and narrated across colonial and national contexts.", "children": [{"id": "e635f2e1-22a2-473e-99c0-10a4ca1423c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Racial formation", "children": [{"id": "9e41a8f2-e64a-4ba8-b9f3-11586a79e6b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial race science"}, {"id": "4ed479b9-4186-4af1-96e5-577404f59751", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and colorism"}, {"id": "36d552ac-2382-40f4-b9f6-76ea4f3f0d79", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnicity"}, {"id": "7797796a-473a-431a-afec-b5e711ea1a09", "type": "concept", "title": "Whiteness"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn that race is historically made through law, labor, science, culture, and representation."}, {"id": "c907003f-5290-40c5-ab9b-5d5f74155042", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative methods", "children": [{"id": "6228fd61-74c4-4dbd-8f6e-0c64f73eaed1", "type": "concept", "title": "Diasporic comparison"}, {"id": "15a9afbf-66d3-40c1-bf63-0e9fc6a1ac80", "type": "concept", "title": "Local specificity"}, {"id": "6562b6c8-4321-4f15-b69f-fb41e40dd3c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared structures"}, {"id": "966a9ee4-6342-467a-9322-fc31ef28f8e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Different archives"}], "description": "Gist: The course compares racialization without assuming all racial histories work the same way."}, {"id": "ac7f540e-7aae-4c05-b0ad-b35a318bfd72", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literary strategies of race critique", "children": [{"id": "5a18baa6-2c59-4c2a-9601-823516c8f3e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Passing"}, {"id": "6f7a4717-ff4f-417f-8d0a-69568951aa09", "type": "concept", "title": "Double consciousness"}, {"id": "41389221-4bbe-42dc-8b0f-ea1a762c02e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Satire"}, {"id": "c4d985a6-efde-4d17-bd31-b62fd32bc77c", "type": "concept", "title": "Testimony"}], "description": "Gist: Writers expose racial power through irony, voice, point of view, realism, and formal rupture."}]}, {"id": "e1d98e7c-c88d-4fcd-be97-ac585a3ee5ea", "type": "topic", "title": "Diaspora, Exile, Migration, and Transnational Identity", "description": "Explore movement, displacement, return, memory, and belonging across borders.", "children": [{"id": "2e8ade19-299b-44ed-b5da-da199a4cdfbc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diaspora as history and method", "children": [{"id": "a1cefc2d-79b9-48d1-9b82-1e48017323b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Homeland"}, {"id": "b59d6f36-c8bc-4500-9b33-0fd6e9684a86", "type": "concept", "title": "Return"}, {"id": "de68f9a8-09d4-4db7-b838-8fa82847d2d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Community"}, {"id": "45745f40-9622-4c6e-9b5a-40fb1f775ef7", "type": "concept", "title": "Generational memory"}], "description": "Gist: Diaspora frames belonging through routes, memory, kinship, and connection across borders."}, {"id": "1a93e60f-52d9-42ab-ace6-35ebfd42be77", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration narratives", "children": [{"id": "a332ac9c-6122-413a-97cb-271544716cf1", "type": "concept", "title": "Exile"}, {"id": "a4d663b9-fb3d-49ca-9cec-116fd20ec232", "type": "concept", "title": "Refugee experience"}, {"id": "fce9b554-3f21-410f-8dd1-bc236852d94f", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor migration"}, {"id": "48126ee9-8a11-4f6e-a677-abcefa6b8549", "type": "concept", "title": "Assimilation and refusal"}], "description": "Gist: Students examine how migration changes language, labor, family, identity, and narrative time."}, {"id": "886efce8-dfdd-421a-97bb-41e9e6150134", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transnational identity", "children": [{"id": "c7356bcc-939d-4427-bcb2-c71f710e2857", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybridity"}, {"id": "6ef54c92-0184-4c72-b372-0b4cec46d850", "type": "concept", "title": "Cosmopolitanism"}, {"id": "7a09965e-c686-4652-a36a-bdb5abb08b4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Remittance cultures"}, {"id": "76aba01d-d526-413d-bd72-bbb9a91adeea", "type": "concept", "title": "Border-crossing"}], "description": "Gist: Texts often unsettle single-nation identity by showing layered and mobile affiliations."}]}, {"id": "4c0a3518-bb28-43ee-accc-c5f477827730", "type": "topic", "title": "Indigeneity, Sovereignty, and Settler Colonialism", "description": "Foreground Indigenous literary sovereignty, land, relation, survivance, and critiques of settler colonial structures.", "children": [{"id": "9e966232-7f1d-45c0-8ce0-53515860f1e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Settler colonial structures", "children": [{"id": "1e5c8995-ad67-4518-82d7-7e414fe4d80d", "type": "concept", "title": "Land seizure"}, {"id": "6de4effa-dd18-4698-8b23-b33e10f3028e", "type": "concept", "title": "Elimination"}, {"id": "49d06b2b-ae8c-421e-83d6-5b74e57d7448", "type": "concept", "title": "Reservation and removal"}, {"id": "557adc40-09a3-407a-a423-7b1be239baec", "type": "concept", "title": "Resource extraction"}], "description": "Gist: Students distinguish settler colonialism from other imperial forms and study its ongoing relation to land."}, {"id": "7dce0020-9024-4d52-b5d4-b0b66d78c08e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous literary sovereignty", "children": [{"id": "8cb30563-72e3-4644-9613-3ef4b48e91ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Survivance"}, {"id": "99e5fced-27b5-4e05-8b60-cb3df246a734", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral tradition"}, {"id": "4f17c6c1-56e3-4cb6-b8dd-8f127b7eb71e", "type": "concept", "title": "Tribal specificity"}, {"id": "9f6c09b7-4ab3-4dae-a38f-2544a1753110", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationality"}], "description": "Gist: Indigenous texts assert continuance, relation, language, law, and self-representation."}, {"id": "8b6144d8-c2b9-416c-bd02-f26b154b9e71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of reading Indigenous texts", "children": [{"id": "34746a24-f4a0-424c-80c7-7b6321321373", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural protocol"}, {"id": "431e16b9-ae3f-46cd-917b-e099b1481a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred knowledge"}, {"id": "a7cb3882-db82-4394-9f17-4f22df7fdfac", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation ethics"}, {"id": "218eede8-f04c-49d0-a438-633e6b573707", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-based reading"}], "description": "Gist: Students attend to protocols, community context, and the limits of outsider interpretation."}]}, {"id": "a5032d01-3370-44bf-92c2-128e9e16a937", "type": "topic", "title": "Black Atlantic and Transnational Frameworks", "description": "Read Black diasporic literature through slavery, modernity, music, memory, migration, and oceanic connection.", "children": [{"id": "4841d95f-b4f9-400a-b599-79c740ad083c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Black Atlantic as analytic frame", "children": [{"id": "6a748ebf-5e6d-4e47-94a7-d1b4e4785c42", "type": "concept", "title": "Middle Passage"}, {"id": "d0e46d65-c7b1-4617-8470-0e9c181924f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation modernity"}, {"id": "cc42ce03-dc53-434f-ac7e-dfa6f3e24f11", "type": "concept", "title": "Maritime routes"}, {"id": "84b0d7d9-2e78-4521-b865-d5c0f9e37e74", "type": "concept", "title": "Diasporic memory"}], "description": "Gist: The Atlantic becomes a space for studying slavery, modernity, movement, and cultural exchange."}, {"id": "bd8db53e-90d9-4771-b7a4-3b253e7567d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music, oral form, and performance", "children": [{"id": "8f8204f7-cfe7-4877-b2ad-a20c703dcab5", "type": "concept", "title": "Call and response"}, {"id": "9175275f-673b-40fd-aebc-250269edcf88", "type": "concept", "title": "Blues and jazz forms"}, {"id": "5e99e154-5582-4b26-9611-211752caba17", "type": "concept", "title": "Spoken word"}, {"id": "9d929eea-6e41-4fbb-aaa5-0d230928d458", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance voice"}], "description": "Gist: Black diasporic literature often carries musical, oral, and performative structures."}, {"id": "dd09c841-d8d3-4b3e-929c-1cf5d33a44a2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freedom, citizenship, and afterlives", "children": [{"id": "a3c6f74d-fd14-4395-b59c-36ec5d8e5f15", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition"}, {"id": "8e7c92b6-9ad6-4c4c-8f9e-d4e4c70fdfbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship"}, {"id": "6f0d5185-8491-4af6-ba4a-11945864e480", "type": "concept", "title": "Carceral power"}, {"id": "fe14419c-3fce-4065-9cc4-30a196476fad", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory of slavery"}], "description": "Gist: Students connect literary form to emancipation, rights, surveillance, and continuing racial violence."}]}, {"id": "62d87a86-5c73-4627-8454-81c11d304027", "type": "topic", "title": "Borderlands, Multilingualism, and Translation", "description": "Study border writing, code-switching, translation, and multilingual form as literary and political practice.", "children": [{"id": "5a14a56d-1956-4de5-abd2-06ad020fc586", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Borderlands theory", "children": [{"id": "de4ff25c-9715-4553-90ba-f76918789a13", "type": "concept", "title": "Border crossing"}, {"id": "c79451f8-c06c-4b93-85e9-47273222ef2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mestiza consciousness"}, {"id": "8fc73717-18c5-4dac-ba7d-baceed9ea0a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Liminality"}, {"id": "4bcd94fe-601b-4ea7-b094-7d2d223edba4", "type": "concept", "title": "State violence"}], "description": "Gist: Border texts show identity as negotiated through geography, law, language, and cultural mixture."}, {"id": "44f8be2d-d0e3-4ff0-a72f-542d3e32043a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multilingual literary practice", "children": [{"id": "874f54df-1b2d-4db0-b7c4-3702a8598984", "type": "concept", "title": "Code-switching"}, {"id": "280e0ed2-d366-4df3-bb07-a7ac94305146", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation within text"}, {"id": "ae2c8e11-d465-4e2b-aa02-a5226cb2ce79", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialect"}, {"id": "ed393660-832a-452d-b9d1-f7fbd5b64ff1", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslated speech"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn how multilingualism changes audience, rhythm, intimacy, and authority."}, {"id": "3f175f7c-db8a-4744-84a5-faa17c1ca1e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation as interpretation", "children": [{"id": "87fba523-3483-4fb9-81e5-3c9062d173f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Translator visibility"}, {"id": "082bfc3e-42be-4dcf-ace8-2e34f0d4465c", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatability"}, {"id": "925af0b8-515a-4dd2-baf2-3f65571ed688", "type": "concept", "title": "World-literary circulation"}, {"id": "50f6b1e7-634b-40a9-9331-67927093e5ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing translations"}], "description": "Gist: Translation is studied as a creative and political act, not just neutral transfer."}]}, {"id": "5dc8bbd4-80f6-4203-a108-255e28f0acf0", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Sexuality, Family, and Embodiment in Postcolonial Contexts", "description": "Analyze how gender, sexuality, kinship, care, and the body shape colonial and postcolonial experience.", "children": [{"id": "6d154e8c-3e9f-4faa-81f5-331351f6f04a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and colonial rule", "children": [{"id": "59521eae-0aee-4b92-b26b-49548c53d7c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectability"}, {"id": "d25d62d0-fb86-4b46-b501-2e404fd176ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Domesticity"}, {"id": "b3cc54cf-b516-4586-9d7f-bcd0fb6812e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Law and marriage"}, {"id": "b4d7bbcc-deb3-4a13-ac9b-e33fcd5cb785", "type": "concept", "title": "Education"}], "description": "Gist: Colonial and nationalist projects often regulate gender, sexuality, reproduction, and family."}, {"id": "222309b5-370a-4a7c-814f-296db0f199e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer and feminist postcolonial critique", "children": [{"id": "1b3a7ac8-2f68-4702-9f32-93da64bee7c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer diaspora"}, {"id": "d5cae6d2-586f-4adc-b902-aae189aee2d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist recovery"}, {"id": "c37d7b4b-f3cb-4e2e-aadd-3db8e0a0b774", "type": "concept", "title": "Masculinity"}, {"id": "1e6e5fa1-a1aa-4f7d-b948-ba8c892f9a2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality"}], "description": "Gist: Students examine how writers challenge patriarchy, heteronormativity, empire, and nationalism together."}, {"id": "48112273-3d81-42aa-a351-145385fe6a67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment and care", "children": [{"id": "7f7aa2b8-79b2-40af-9ee5-31f641d4df6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodied memory"}, {"id": "2af9518a-66eb-4fe0-a1f1-e5771952819c", "type": "concept", "title": "Food and care"}, {"id": "25a9191e-99ad-4cf4-ad75-e7a99ab55b93", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual violence"}, {"id": "75b72145-0ed0-4d47-b48a-f0c8838c92d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Healing practices"}], "description": "Gist: The body records labor, violence, illness, desire, survival, and intergenerational care."}]}, {"id": "a407df5f-606c-4204-910d-d9e443c63472", "type": "topic", "title": "Memory, Trauma, Testimony, and Archive", "description": "Examine how literature remembers violence, preserves testimony, and contests archival absence.", "children": [{"id": "30f36e93-1411-4811-8133-8350bd4b7f84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literature as testimony", "children": [{"id": "fcaff281-3f10-444b-8d70-1f40c1486f6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Witness"}, {"id": "96cbd47a-0135-4117-ae3c-118da16d81f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Survivor narrative"}, {"id": "3bb78cd9-5872-422f-84b3-60b4a23fa5e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral history"}, {"id": "de5e33bf-9e1f-4861-ba3d-ba40e4d1f399", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary form"}], "description": "Gist: Texts can preserve experience that law, state history, or official archives distort or ignore."}, {"id": "76e0c9a0-0595-4887-9a83-974ca08258c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Trauma and narrative form", "children": [{"id": "bd9f7836-4c0f-427e-a224-0cf2ecf2732d", "type": "concept", "title": "Silence"}, {"id": "5a3ee7f0-f40a-4bc9-b7f6-62dcf655f73d", "type": "concept", "title": "Flashback"}, {"id": "f8c450ad-3aba-4c53-9fc7-14a6663292aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Repetition"}, {"id": "910a3744-3cac-49ba-bf34-962733b73e54", "type": "concept", "title": "Unreliable memory"}], "description": "Gist: Fragmented, recursive, or nonlinear form may register violence and memory's difficulty."}, {"id": "64d31c1e-5c25-40bb-8fd7-d3201312ec8f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archive and counter-archive", "children": [{"id": "bce6b872-3e81-4562-ae20-ae3109ab116d", "type": "concept", "title": "Ephemera"}, {"id": "5434285b-ef60-4b42-be36-ac9c67e38815", "type": "concept", "title": "Family archive"}, {"id": "d28cee37-3107-4037-b9b5-99cbd40fe8f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Photographs"}, {"id": "862b6061-829d-47ee-b838-6c5904f85d9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Archival absence"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn how writers build alternative archives through memory, objects, family stories, and community record."}]}, {"id": "187c3f4f-f56b-40e9-9051-4a81d8ddc81d", "type": "topic", "title": "Genre, Form, and Aesthetic Innovation", "description": "Track how writers adapt realism, epic, lyric, drama, memoir, speculative fiction, and experimental form.", "children": [{"id": "55556b6b-16fe-4f1c-9318-943c598cf72c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Realism and its revisions", "children": [{"id": "b3828c22-c11d-4069-a20c-3158e723dc4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Social realism"}, {"id": "411e246d-d891-46e2-9688-8ea5388fee92", "type": "concept", "title": "Magical realism"}, {"id": "c8d0075c-9c24-4e1a-8b81-2d332b3608ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Autofiction"}, {"id": "f3d7eea1-014e-4b21-a0c6-b5632f07081e", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary realism"}], "description": "Gist: Realism can document social worlds while postcolonial writers also stretch its limits."}, {"id": "2567ff8c-c2b3-4c86-bc3c-06eacaaa1b9a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Speculative and experimental forms", "children": [{"id": "60e97a00-468d-480e-b77c-39f9a58615f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Afrofuturism"}, {"id": "cf98667b-5592-4fa4-a1a0-46730cffbc59", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate fiction"}, {"id": "6b4c6288-a65c-404c-a453-0efb7146a6ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragmentation"}, {"id": "11a9914c-bb18-42f0-b84f-a8bab6e0aded", "type": "concept", "title": "Polyphony"}], "description": "Gist: Speculative and experimental writing makes colonial histories and alternative futures newly visible."}, {"id": "0dd77994-f75b-4935-8c89-a97de679f267", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry, drama, and performance", "children": [{"id": "fef81ba8-f082-42a2-87fd-39955b8016c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Lyric"}, {"id": "ddb14917-80a8-4ac0-a71a-6c1ec80c89ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Epic"}, {"id": "314f9611-50e5-46e2-8a43-6e27a337f10c", "type": "concept", "title": "Testimonial theater"}, {"id": "a4c0f128-ed8d-4b4e-a08f-3ba624997c2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance poetry"}], "description": "Gist: Non-novel forms foreground voice, rhythm, embodiment, public memory, and collective address."}]}, {"id": "1fb72458-23a5-43a0-9403-daad227cd3cb", "type": "topic", "title": "World Literature, Global Markets, and Prize Culture", "description": "Interrogate how texts circulate through translation, publishing, prizes, syllabi, and global literary markets.", "children": [{"id": "abbf7905-497c-407c-8598-96aba3286042", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World literature debates", "children": [{"id": "a2385e92-2f68-48e3-8ee2-647861ad416c", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon expansion"}, {"id": "47d2e2c4-65f4-4c6c-9826-5466bc51f0d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation markets"}, {"id": "4ad807e0-6df0-4ff0-b89e-5d9b43dca2bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative reading"}, {"id": "ec8aff70-f47b-4a23-b478-996abc15a827", "type": "concept", "title": "Global English"}], "description": "Gist: Students ask what it means for texts to circulate globally and what gets lost or gained in circulation."}, {"id": "5fc15120-3fd0-417b-b8d3-e4571691547e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Publishing and prize culture", "children": [{"id": "2581a102-bdb6-490e-88f5-22a98eeb5aaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Book prizes"}, {"id": "aa6c0814-6630-45f0-abdc-8b9d9312c33f", "type": "concept", "title": "Anthologies"}, {"id": "58268c9e-07d9-4f2f-ac25-96bf94377bcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Marketing categories"}, {"id": "72deac0f-46a7-43fd-9133-a0a9b43113a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Academic canon"}], "description": "Gist: The course examines how prizes, publishers, reviews, and syllabi shape literary value."}, {"id": "d5969284-260b-4722-bcd1-bba330d3bd3b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading against market flattening", "children": [{"id": "e4c523a5-86c7-492f-b343-6661f3f706a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Local context"}, {"id": "ad569840-1a38-47a7-8eda-ac29d1cbaba7", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception"}, {"id": "a3bc49ad-6bdd-432b-be86-80a2393a8faf", "type": "concept", "title": "Exoticization"}, {"id": "430cc2da-3425-4f3e-b8e9-e26151077c72", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible comparison"}], "description": "Gist: Students practice preserving formal and historical specificity when texts become global commodities."}]}, {"id": "d3db3e07-4221-4e45-a2db-59c1f3e47af5", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Theory and Methods", "description": "Introduce postcolonial, decolonial, ethnic studies, feminist, queer, Marxist, and formalist methods as tools for interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "54fcff28-28db-4853-bade-a21c93d6608f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial and decolonial theory", "children": [{"id": "b09aaa76-3f23-47b2-9639-d8f641793de7", "type": "concept", "title": "Said"}, {"id": "629554f2-1087-4e47-83f1-7254a3e70b69", "type": "concept", "title": "Bhabha"}, {"id": "ddba8360-e445-4016-9a4e-58f471b60411", "type": "concept", "title": "Spivak"}, {"id": "3e3e57e7-099e-45b8-87ed-0af18b02241c", "type": "concept", "title": "Decoloniality"}], "description": "Gist: Theory helps students ask how empire shapes knowledge, culture, subjectivity, and institutions."}, {"id": "d5734908-dd0f-488c-9320-5c5dfa7cfa86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnic studies and critical race approaches", "children": [{"id": "92e3a882-41c8-4027-9e35-031b1f23f672", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative ethnic studies"}, {"id": "48d90800-7339-4b7b-ac6a-258f884cb9b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical race theory"}, {"id": "09ef0182-6df2-4501-9b11-a9a9391962bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora studies"}, {"id": "ca2604da-7af8-4916-a393-38effc57f757", "type": "concept", "title": "Settler colonial critique"}], "description": "Gist: These methods connect literary form to racial formation, migration, law, labor, and community history."}, {"id": "6aefba37-552a-4c2c-ae7a-488e5b04482c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Method in practice", "children": [{"id": "f52a71c0-90cf-4ec1-b0fb-860ae6201797", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept definition"}, {"id": "3eeeb4bd-afba-4a26-9153-2548474cf010", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence selection"}, {"id": "eb0c95bc-e4a0-4d1c-a790-c876ca8eaaa8", "type": "concept", "title": "Method limits"}, {"id": "54c40c7a-878a-40d9-958a-6230399316a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing readings"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn to apply theory through passages rather than dropping jargon onto texts."}]}, {"id": "79f28b51-ba3c-480c-9b22-705ef3266acb", "type": "topic", "title": "Research, Secondary Sources, and Scholarly Ethics", "description": "Build responsible research habits for working with criticism, archives, historical context, and community-sensitive materials.", "children": [{"id": "ba3afc08-b1ae-430c-b39a-e2c1d360755b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Finding scholarly conversations", "children": [{"id": "64147756-e680-4aef-a86b-0675c725d84b", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA International Bibliography"}, {"id": "3d07a058-7a96-4783-adfd-626ea134e5c5", "type": "concept", "title": "JSTOR"}, {"id": "fd583af2-6a85-4bb9-9f89-dec97d16dc26", "type": "concept", "title": "Project MUSE"}, {"id": "e7466edf-c665-4942-a651-fba3bd84ef81", "type": "concept", "title": "Oxford Bibliographies"}], "description": "Gist: Students use research tools to locate field-specific criticism and debates."}, {"id": "04461a4b-4245-4f7d-8715-6484eb9b6a9a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Using sources ethically", "children": [{"id": "e6a41382-c982-4b94-b0ab-f5c574059da8", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation"}, {"id": "dd2da2b3-be84-48b6-bc16-faa972a73509", "type": "concept", "title": "Paraphrase"}, {"id": "ee6ba6cf-8a49-4d44-8064-6b9048c468a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Context"}, {"id": "567bf0f7-d103-499a-b63c-04807cb897d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding extraction"}], "description": "Gist: Students learn to represent scholars, communities, and histories carefully."}, {"id": "e9cd773e-a239-4d78-8041-3e746325f37b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "bbc179e4-f5b9-4302-afab-ff277b875097", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question"}, {"id": "3b5f0bc5-65a8-46eb-ab90-1d32a5d53981", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}, {"id": "7518f502-7939-4543-80a4-ef0e44f66b6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Source synthesis"}, {"id": "577bd7ba-dbf9-4952-8023-cc58a90bbb72", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision plan"}], "description": "Gist: A strong project joins close reading, theory, historical context, and a focused question."}]}, {"id": "71656415-9be8-4ea3-b254-319cbea775bb", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Literary Analysis Project", "description": "Synthesize close reading, theory, context, research, and comparative argument in a final literary analysis project.", "children": [{"id": "ba09ed47-829a-4749-b5b0-3872f49286f4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Building a comparative claim", "children": [{"id": "185bfabb-70f8-4023-8458-d80b178f908e", "type": "concept", "title": "Ground of comparison"}, {"id": "e2fa55e0-f7ab-4fc3-a37e-654386ffc431", "type": "concept", "title": "Difference and relation"}, {"id": "b8b1455d-f034-4b4b-b2b1-a80836a536c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope"}, {"id": "29f63b39-c73c-40d1-8db3-624d48c02e87", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakes"}], "description": "Gist: Students develop arguments that compare texts for a reason, not just side-by-side similarity."}, {"id": "759db268-9a37-43cf-bc40-b946cc022b0b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrating close reading and context", "children": [{"id": "5e6f1367-291a-47a8-a7ad-9b58cb981ccf", "type": "concept", "title": "Passage analysis"}, {"id": "ddb8248d-559f-4f87-beb0-934d6c713d86", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical framing"}, {"id": "0eae90a9-35c9-4c15-9f5b-39a77d5ec8cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory use"}, {"id": "b8a0342b-cd8a-445b-9c73-4ffa4ad7f334", "type": "concept", "title": "Secondary sources"}], "description": "Gist: The final project shows how textual details and historical context illuminate each other."}, {"id": "16d2422e-fee9-4b6f-986f-7439bdacef33", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision and presentation", "children": [{"id": "95ed533a-5bd1-4b3f-99b1-56867ddd7fa3", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer review"}, {"id": "700c04b4-687d-46fa-a6a6-9d2aabdcfa8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Reverse outline"}, {"id": "901c8a78-ca6c-4f3a-aa3e-077fb0e4a1e0", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA Works Cited"}, {"id": "c83ca605-7455-41dc-9b7c-03a20704fd03", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral presentation"}], "description": "Gist: Students refine claims, structure, citation, and scholarly voice before presenting their work."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$mpocjson$::jsonb,
