guish settler colonialism from other imperial forms and study its ongoing relation to land."}, {"id": "7dce0020-9024-4d52-b5d4-b0b66d78c08e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous literary sovereignty", "children": [{"id": "8cb30563-72e3-4644-9613-3ef4b48e91ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Survivance"}, {"id": "99e5fced-27b5-4e05-8b60-cb3df246a734", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral tradition"}, {"id": "4f17c6c1-56e3-4cb6-b8dd-8f127b7eb71e", "type": "concept", "title": "Tribal specificity"}, {"id": "9f6c09b7-4ab3-4dae-a38f-2544a1753110", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationality"}], "description": "Gist: Indigenous texts assert continuance, relation, language, law, and self-representation."}, {"id": "8b6144d8-c2b9-416c-bd02-f26b154b9e71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of reading Indigenous texts", "children": [{"id": "34746a24-f4a0-424c-80c7-7b6321321373", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural protocol"}, {"id": "431e16b9-ae3f-46cd-917b-e099b1481a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred knowledge"}, {"id": "a7cb3882-db82-4394-9f17-4f22df7fdfac", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation ethics"}, {"id": "218eede8-f04c-49d0-a438-633e6b573707", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-based reading"}], "description": "Gist: Students attend to protocols, community context, and the limits of outsider interpretation."}]}, {"id": "a5032d01-3370-44bf-92c2-128e9e16a937", "type": "topic", "title": "Black Atlantic and Transnational Frameworks", "description": "Read Black diasporic literature through slavery, modernity, music, memory, migration, and oceanic connection.", "children": [{"id": "4841d95f-b4f9-400a-b599-79c740ad083c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Black Atlantic as analytic frame", "children": [{"id": "6a748ebf-5e6d-4e47-94a7-d1b4e4785c42", "type": "concept", "title": "Middle Passage"}, {"id": "d0e46d65-c7b1-4617-8470-0e9c181924f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation modernity"}, {"id": "cc42ce03-dc53-434f-ac7e-dfa6f3e24f11", "type": "concept", "title": "Maritime routes"}, {"id": "84b0d7d9-2e78-4521-b865-d5c0f9e37e74", "type": "concept", "title": "Diasporic memory"}], "description": "Gist: The Atlantic becomes a space for studying slavery, modernity, movement, and cultural exchange."}, {"id": "bd8db53e-90d9-4771-b7a4-3b253e7567d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music, oral form, and performance", "children": [{"id": "8f8204f7-cfe7-4877-b2ad-a20c703dcab5", "type": "concept", "title": "Call and response"}, {"id": "9175275f-673b-40fd-aebc-250269edcf88", "type": "concept", "title": "Blues and jazz forms"}, {"id": "5e99e154-5582-4b26-9611-211752caba17", "type": "concept", "title": "Spoken word"}, {"id": "9d929eea-6e41-4fbb-aaa5-0d230928d458", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance voice"}], "description": "Gist: Black diasporic literature often carries musical, oral, and performative structures."}, {"id": "dd09c841-d8d3-4b3e-929c-1cf5d33a44a2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Freedom, citizenship, and afterlives", "children": [{"id": "a3c6f74d-fd14-4395-b59c-36ec5d8e5f15", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition"}, {"id": "8e7c92b6-9ad6-4c4c-8f9e-d4e4c70fdfbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship"}, {"id": "6f0d5185-8491-4af6-ba4a-11945864e480", "type": "concept", "title": "Carceral power"}, {"id": "fe14419c-3fce-4065-9cc4-30a196476fad", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory of slavery"}], "description": "Gist: Students connect literary form to emancipation, rights, surveillance, and continuing racial violence."}]}, {"id": "62d87a86-5c73-4627-8454-81c11d304027", "type": "topic", "title": "Borderlands, Multilingualism, and Translation", "description": "Study border writing, code-switching, translation, and multilingual form as literary and political practice.", "children": [{"id": "5a14a56d-1956-4de5-abd2-06ad020fc586", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Borderlands theory", "children": [{"id": "de4ff25c-9715-4553-90ba-f76918789a13", "type": "concept", "title": "Border crossing"}, {"id": "c79451f8-c06c-4b93-85e9-47273222ef2d", 